 saying that they've lied I  think you run the risk that you look  like you are being you're not being  objective and I think  and I do think also it applies the  people this is this is happening all the  time now people are looking at what  don't some saying said this is false  suppose claim I think people say well  you know what Hillary Clinton said a lot  of things were false I don't recall the  press being quite so quite so concerned  about saying that she lied so what  Hillary Clinton has to do with it I have  no idea we're now nearly two months past  the election of Donald Trump Hillary  Clinton is nowhere to be found no clue  what she has to do with any of this so  Gerard Baker's making this into more of  a semantics and definition thing right  the word lie includes an intent you know  it's not true and you say it anyway  presumably to mislead or deceive versus  you miss stated a fact which could come  from ignorance you can't know the intent  so you can't call them lies but will  report inaccuracies not bad right well  no not really what Gerard Baker is  suggesting is pussyfooting around we  have not found evidence that what Donald  Trump alleges is true Donald Trump's  lies at our side are at such a level  that this is not going to cut it and  other media outlets have not hesitated  to call them lies the New York Times  MSNBC a New York Magazine The Washington  Post oh well they don't matter because  they're just liberal serious media like  The Wall Street Journal which is by the  way owned by News Corporation which is  the company that owns Fox News as well  they are going to be better because we  don't know what Donald Trump's intent is  when Donald Trump claims that the  unemployment rate is really 42 percent  when it's five percent we're going to  say we've not been able to find evidence  that the unemployment rate is 42 percent  and let the public decide when Donald  Trump claimed with no evidence that he  could save 300 billion dollars a year  from th